 {
  "skill": "cx-conjugate-root",
  "problems": [
   {
    "question": "The real polynomial $z^3 + 4z^2 + 9z + 10$ has $-1 + 2i$ as a root.\\nFind the other two roots.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Use the conjugate root theorem",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "The coefficients are real, so complex roots come in conjugate pairs."
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\overline{-1 + 2i} &= -1 - 2i",
         "sym": "Eq(conjugate(-1 + 2*I), -1 - 2*I)",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Find the quadratic factor from the pair",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "Sum of the pair, then product of the pair."
        },
        {
         "tex": "(-1 + 2i) + (-1 - 2i) &= -2",
         "sym": "Eq((-1 + 2*I) + (-1 - 2*I), -2)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "(-1 + 2i)(-1 - 2i) &= 1 - 4i^2 = 5",
         "sym": "Eq(expand((-1 + 2*I)*(-1 - 2*I)), 5)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "So the quadratic factor is $z^2 + 2z + 5$."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Divide to find the linear factor",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "Compare constant terms: $5 \\times (\\text{constant}) = 10$, so the constant is $2$."
        },
        {
         "tex": "z^3 + 4z^2 + 9z + 10 &= (z^2 + 2z + 5)(z + 2)",
         "sym": "Eq(z**3 + 4*z**2 + 9*z + 10, expand((z**2 + 2*z + 5)*(z + 2)))",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Read off the third root",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "z + 2 &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq(z + 2, 0)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "z &= -2",
         "sym": "Eq(z, -2)"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "z = -1 - 2i,\\ -2"
    },
    "finalSym": "[-1 - 2*I, -2]",
    "answerSym": "solve(Eq((z**3 + 4*z**2 + 9*z + 10)/(z - (-1 + 2*I)), 0), z)"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "skill": "diff-curve-sketch",
  "problems": [
   {
    "question": "Find the coordinates of the local maximum and the local minimum of $y = x^3 - 12x$.\\nUse the second derivative to say which is which.",
    "level": "ordinary",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Differentiate",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "y &= x^3 - 12x"
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 3x^2 - 12",
         "sym": "3*x**2 - 12",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Set $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "3x^2 - 12 &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq(3*x**2 - 12, 0)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "x^2 - 4 &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq(x**2 - 4, 0)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq((x - 2)*(x + 2), 0)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -2",
         "sym": "Or(Eq(x, 2), Eq(x, -2))"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Find the $y$-values",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "x = 2: \\quad y &= 8 - 24 = -16",
         "sym": "8 - 24",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "x = -2: \\quad y &= -8 + 24 = 16",
         "sym": "-8 + 24",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Classify using the second derivative",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} &= 6x",
         "sym": "6*x",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "At $x = -2$: $-12 < 0$, so $(-2, 16)$ is a local maximum."
        },
        {
         "text": "At $x = 2$: $12 > 0$, so $(2, -16)$ is a local minimum."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "\\text{Local max } (-2, 16); \\ \\text{local min } (2, -16)"
    },
    "finalSym": "[(-2, 16), (2, -16)]",
    "answerSym": "solve([Eq(diff(x**3 - 12*x, x), 0), Eq(y, x**3 - 12*x)], [x, y])"
   },
   {
    "question": "Find the coordinates of the local maximum and the local minimum of $y = -x^3 + 3x^2 + 9x$.\\nUse the second derivative to say which is which.",
    "level": "ordinary",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Differentiate",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "y &= -x^3 + 3x^2 + 9x"
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -3x^2 + 6x + 9",
         "sym": "-3*x**2 + 6*x + 9",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Set $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "-3x^2 + 6x + 9 &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq(-3*x**2 + 6*x + 9, 0)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "x^2 - 2x - 3 &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq(x**2 - 2*x - 3, 0)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "(x - 3)(x + 1) &= 0",
         "sym": "Eq((x - 3)*(x + 1), 0)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1",
         "sym": "Or(Eq(x, 3), Eq(x, -1))"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Find the $y$-values",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "x = 3: \\quad y &= -27 + 27 + 27 = 27",
         "sym": "-27 + 27 + 27",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "x = -1: \\quad y &= 1 + 3 - 9 = -5",
         "sym": "1 + 3 - 9",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Classify using the second derivative",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} &= -6x + 6",
         "sym": "-6*x + 6",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "At $x = 3$: $-18 + 6 = -12 < 0$, so $(3, 27)$ is a local maximum."
        },
        {
         "text": "At $x = -1$: $6 + 6 = 12 > 0$, so $(-1, -5)$ is a local minimum."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "\\text{Local max } (3, 27); \\ \\text{local min } (-1, -5)"
    },
    "finalSym": "[(3, 27), (-1, -5)]",
    "answerSym": "solve([Eq(diff(-x**3 + 3*x**2 + 9*x, x), 0), Eq(y, -x**3 + 3*x**2 + 9*x)], [x, y])"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "skill": "diff-point-given-slope",
  "problems": [
   {
    "question": "A curve has equation $y = x^2 + 4x - 1$.\\nFind the point on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the line $2x + y = 5$.",
    "level": "ordinary",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Find the slope of the line",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "2x + y &= 5",
         "sym": "Eq(2*x + y, 5)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "y &= -2x + 5",
         "sym": "Eq(y, -2*x + 5)"
        },
        {
         "text": "The line has slope $-2$, so the tangent has slope $-2$."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Differentiate the curve",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "y &= x^2 + 4x - 1",
         "sym": "x**2 + 4*x - 1"
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x + 4",
         "sym": "2*x + 4",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Set the slope equal to $-2$ and solve",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "2x + 4 &= -2",
         "sym": "Eq(2*x + 4, -2)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "2x &= -6",
         "sym": "Eq(2*x, -6)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= -3",
         "sym": "Eq(x, -3)"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Find y at this x-value",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "y &= (-3)^2 + 4(-3) - 1",
         "sym": "(-3)**2 + 4*(-3) - 1",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "&= -4",
         "sym": "-4"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "(-3, -4)"
    },
    "finalSym": "(-3, -4)",
    "answerSym": "solve([Eq(diff(x**2 + 4*x - 1, x), -2), Eq(y, x**2 + 4*x - 1)], [x, y])"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "skill": "diff-sketch-derivative",
  "problems": [
   {
    "question": "$f(x)$ is a cubic function with a local maximum at $x = 1$ and a local minimum at $x = 5$.\\nThe slope of $f(x)$ at its point of inflection is $-4$.\\nWrite down the coordinates of three points on the graph of $f'(x)$ that you would plot to sketch it.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 5,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Turning points of $f$ give the roots of $f'$",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "At a turning point the slope of $f$ is $0$, so $f'(1) = 0$ and $f'(5) = 0$."
        },
        {
         "text": "The graph of $f'$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(1, 0)$ and $(5, 0)$."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Use the point of inflection",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$f$ is a cubic, so $f'$ is a quadratic: a U-shape, with its lowest point halfway between its roots."
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= \\frac{1 + 5}{2} = 3",
         "sym": "Eq(x, Rational(1 + 5, 2))",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "This is the inflection point of $f$, where the slope is least, and it is given as $-4$: $f'(3) = -4$."
        },
        {
         "text": "The lowest point of the graph of $f'$ is $(3, -4)$."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "(1, 0),\\ (5, 0),\\ (3, -4)"
    },
    "finalSym": "[(1, 0), (5, 0), (3, -4)]",
    "answerSym": "[(1, 0), (5, 0), (Rational(1 + 5, 2), -4)]"
   }
  ]
 }
]